[CREATEPART]
New Partition
=============

With this function, you can create
a partition in a free space.

You can see which partition type
you can use in the Type field of
the selected free space.

The following is generally stated
regarding the selected type:

PRIMARY  Many operating systems can
         only be installed on a
         primary partition (e.g.,
         MS-DOS). Only one primary
         partition per hard drive
         can be "visible," meaning
         it can be accessed via a
         drive letter.

LOGICALLY only one so-called exten-
          ded partition can be
          created per hard disk.
          This partition can then
          be divided into logical
          drives, which are always
          "visible" if the file
          system is supported by
          the respective operating
          system. (For example, DOS
          cannot access a drive
          formatted with OS/2
          HPFS.)
          Therefore, make sure to
          only create logical
          drives in a contiguous
          area.
[ENDKEY]
         1         2         3
|--------+---------+---------+----|
[DELETEPART]

Delete Partition
================

With this function, you can delete
existing partitions. Please note
that the partition may not be
recoverable.

[ENDKEY]
         1         2         3
|--------+---------+---------+----|
[ACTIVATEPART]
Activate Partition
==================

This function allows you to acti-
vate a primary partition. The ac-
tive partition will boot automati-
cally when the computer starts. If
the boot manager is installed, the
default partition will boot auto-
matically, not the active one, if
the user does not select another
partition from the boot menu.
[ENDKEY]
         1         2         3
|--------+---------+---------+----|
[SELECTDRIVE]
Make Partition Visible
======================

Since DOS and OS/2 can only cor-
rectly manage one partition per
connected hard drive, all other
partitions recognized by the re-
spective operating system must be
hidden. Using the "Make Partition
Visible" menu item, you can make a
hidden partition visible to the
operating system again. The re-
maining partitions will be auto-
matically hidden if necessary.
[ENDKEY]
         1         2         3
|--------+---------+---------+----|
[CHANGEPARTTYPE]
Changing the Partition Type
===========================

Typically, each partition and logi-
cal drive on the hard drive is for-
matted to prepare a structure for
managing files and directories on
the hard drive. This structure is
also called a file system.
However, different operating sys-
tems also have different require-
ments for this structure, so many
different file systems exist. DOS
alone has introduced three file
systems to date (FAT12, FAT16,
FAT32). A file system is now asso-
ciated with a partition type, which
is stored in the partition table.
Ideally, a file system corresponds
to a partition type, and vice
versa. Unfortunately, this is often
not the case. For example, the
widely used FAT16 system uses two
partition types: one for partitions
and logical drives smaller than 32
MB, and one for those larger than
32 MB. The partition type used for
the OS/2 file system (HPFS), on the
other hand, is multi-occupied. It
is also used for NTFS (Windows NT)
and QNX.
When you create a partition or
logical drive with XFDisk, FAT16
is automatically assumed as the
file system and the appropriate
partition type is assigned. This
is often appropriate, but if the
partition is not to be formatted
with FAT16, the partition type
must be changed.
Use this menu item carefully and
only if you know what you are do-
ing.
Changing the partition type will
result in the loss of any data that
may be present on the partition or
logical drive!

Important partition types
-------------------------

01 FAT12 <16MB
04 FAT16 <32MB
06 FAT16 >32MB
07 HPFS/NTFS
0B FAT32
0C FAT32 INT13X
0E FAT16 INT13X
82 Linux Swap
83 Linux Native
A5 BSD/386
[ENDKEY]
         1         2         3
|--------+---------+---------+----|
[INSERTENTR]
New Boot Menu Entry
===================

With this function, you can add a
partition to the boot menu and thus
create your own individual boot
configuration.
Using this function, you can also
assign a name that will appear in
the boot menu and can be up to 16
characters long. Choose a name that
is as descriptive as possible; the
choice is entirely up to you.
[ENDKEY]
         1         2         3
|--------+---------+---------+----|
[DELETEENTR]
Delete Boot Menu Entry
======================

With this function, you can remove
a partition listed in the boot
menu.
The partition itself is, of course,
not deleted and can be added back
to the boot menu if necessary.
[ENDKEY]
         1         2         3
|--------+---------+---------+----|
[CHANGENAME]
Change Boot Menu Entry
======================

With this function, you can change
the name you assigned to a par-
tition in the boot menu.
[ENDKEY]
         1         2         3
|--------+---------+---------+----|
[CHANGEPASSWORD]
Set a Password
==============

If you have entered a partition in
the boot menu, you can use this
function to assign a password that
will be automatically requested
when booting via the boot manager.
This makes it possible to restrict
access to your computer.
Write down your password and store
it in a secure location that is in-
accessible to third parties.
Please note that a German keyboard
driver is normally not yet loaded
when the computer starts.
Please read Chapter 2.2 in
XFDISK.TXT for more information.
[ENDKEY]
         1         2         3
|--------+---------+---------+----|
[UNINSTALL]
Uninstalling the boot manager
=============================

The boot manager can also be unin-
stalled. This will result in the
loss of all settings you have made
in the boot menu.
If you reinstall the boot manager,
you will need to reconfigure it.
[ENDKEY]
         1         2         3
|--------+---------+---------+----|
[Bootmanager]
Set Boot Values
===============

With the functions in this submenu,
you can further customize the boot
manager to your needs. You can set
the time the boot manager should
wait for your input before booting
the standard partition, as well as
the passwords for the boot manager
itself and the floppy boot func-
tion.
The standard partition itself can
also be specified via this submenu.

[ENDKEY]
         1         2         3
|--------+---------+---------+----|
[INSTALL]
Installing the Boot Manager
===========================

The boot manager allows you to in-
stall and manage multiple operating
systems on different logical drives
or partitions on one hard drive.
However, the boot manager should
normally only be installed and con-
figured once all systems have been
fully installed.
Using the "New Boot Menu Entry"
menu item, you can add partitions
and logical drives to the boot
menu.
Please note that only certain ope-
rating systems are suitable for
installation on logical drives.
Be sure to also read the infor-
mation in README.TXT.
[ENDKEY]
         1         2         3
|--------+---------+---------+----|
[LOADSAVE]
Load/Save Configuration
=======================

With this function, the partitio-
ning of the connected hard drives
and the boot manager configuration
can be saved to a file or loaded
from a file.
All information is saved, inclu-
ding, for example, whether a par-
tition has been newly created and
therefore needs to be initialized.
You can only load a configuration
file if the information in the file
matches the currently connected
drives. The saved information is
therefore not suitable for instal-
ling a configuration on multiple,
different computers.
Please also read the documentation
for more information.
[ENDKEY]
         1         2         3
|--------+---------+---------+----|
[STARTAKTPART]
Default Partition
=================

With this function, you can set the
currently selected partition as the
so-called default partition and
thus determine which system the
boot manager should automatically
boot if no input is received. You
can set the time the boot manager
should wait for your input using
the "Boot Menu Timeout" function.
[ENDKEY]
         1         2         3
|--------+---------+---------+----|
[STARTLASTPART]
Remember selection
==================

If you enable this option, the boot
manager will remember the selected
partition and use it as the default
partition the next time it boots.
This will not affect the order of
the partitions in the boot menu.
[ENDKEY]
         1         2         3
|--------+---------+---------+----|
[STARTTHISPART]
Select this partition
=====================

Using the "Remember selection" op-
tion, you can specify the partition
to boot by default the next time
the system boots, without changing
the order of the entries in the
boot menu.
[ENDKEY]
         1         2         3
|--------+---------+---------+----|
[STARTTIMEOUT]
Boot Menu Timeout
=================

With this function, you can set the
time the boot manager should wait
for your input before automatically
booting the partition defined as
the default. Valid values are from
10 to 300 seconds, or 0 if the boot
manager should not automatically
boot the default partition.
[ENDKEY]
         1         2         3
|--------+---------+---------+----|
[STARTHANDLING]
Timeout Behavior
================

This option allows you to determine
how the boot manager handles the
timeout when the Up, Down, and F5
keys are pressed.
If "Disable on Keypress"
is selected, the boot manager auto-
matically disables the timeout when
any of the three keys are pressed.
If "Reset on Keypress"
is selected, the timeout is reset
to its original value and starts
again from the beginning.
If "Ignore Keypress"
is selected, the timeout continues
unaffected.
The timeout is always temporarily
suspended while the BIOS screen is
displayed with the F5 key.
The Up and Down keys only affect
the timeout behavior when the key-
strokes are effective, meaning the
selection bar is actually moved up
 or down.
[ENDKEY]
         1         2         3
|--------+---------+---------+----|
[STARTCLRSCR]
Clear Screen
============

This option determines whether the
BIOS screen remains displayed while
the boot manager is displayed, or
whether it should be cleared first.
In either case, the BIOS screen can
be fully displayed using the F5 key
and will be displayed again before
the boot process.
[ENDKEY]
         1         2         3
|--------+---------+---------+----|
[STARTSIMPLE]
Simple Menus
============

This option displays only the par-
tition name in the boot manager.
[ENDKEY]
         1         2         3
|--------+---------+---------+----|
[STARTMASTER]
Set a master password
=====================

The master password is requested
every time the system starts, even
before the boot menu is displayed.
This gives you an additional option
to restrict access to your computer
in conjunction with the BIOS's lock
options.
Please note that a German keyboard
driver is not normally loaded when
the computer starts.
For more information, please read
Chapter 2.2 in XFDISK.TXT.
[ENDKEY]
         1         2         3
|--------+---------+---------+----|
[STARTFLOPPY]
Set Floppy Password
========================

The boot manager offers the option
of booting from a floppy disk des-
pite a changed boot order.
With this function, you can assign
a password to booting from a floppy
disk so that this function does not
become a security risk.
Please note that a German keyboard
driver is normally not loaded when
the computer starts.

For more information, please read
Chapter 2.2 in XFDISK.TXT.
[ENDKEY]
